{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgnx46TuhF2a88mvhc32P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The scale-free test gives strong results for the base-copy heads, with L7H0 and L7H1 far out on both sides. The D-copy values all sit within about 1.35 z, so nothing stands out there. I'm also seeing a weak 0.24 correlation between base-copy z and D-copy z, which hints that copy-heavy heads may lean slightly more positive in D as well, and I'm checking L8 next.\n\nThe anti-copy heads mostly come out negative on the D side, which suggests the A model amplifies their existing structure rather than reshaping it. Layers 16 and 17 and layer 8 are all positive in D-copy, but with heads in a layer sharing their value weights, that pattern looks weak. The OV copy change looks essentially null, so the main behavioral difference probably isn't in OV.\n\nRedoing the QK check with a scale-free test instead: I'll count the fraction of positive diagonal elements of ΔB and use a normalized diagonal z. The earlier QK traces reached about ±14, which may just reflect a few high-gain input dimensions dominating.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5829,"cache_read_input_tokens":229407,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5829},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8eb70499-b3ff-4738-a5c2-a133122d9167","uuid":"9c9ffa0e-bce9-40f7-a03c-2cacecdad527","timestamp":"2026-11-07T04:38:43.888Z","request_id":"req_011Cgnx4443sZ3kETU5R73Si","thinking_duration_ms":7767,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgnx46TuhF2a88mvhc32P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The scale-free test **validates cleanly on the base model**, which makes the null on D much more trustworthy. Copy heads have 95–100% positive diagonals (L7H0 0.99 / z +132, L17H1 0.99 / +131, L15H3 1.00 / +87). Anti-copy heads have 1–5% (L7H1 0.01 / −174, L8H0 0.04 / −143, L9H1 0.01 / −125, L15H1 0.01 / −107). The base MLPs also have strong signatures.\n\nFor the differences, every head and MLP has a positive fraction of 0.45–0.55 and |z| ≤ 1.35. So **neither model copies more or less than the other through any OV circuit or MLP**, under any positive diagonal metric. There are two faint regularities:\n- all 8 heads of layers 16–17 have z > 0 (+0.28 to +1.24);\n- across heads, corr(base copy z, D copy z) = +0.24, i.e. A's existing copy structure is very slightly accentuated.\n\nBoth are too weak to use. Probability A is sycophantic: 0.45.\n\nThis strengthens the conclusion that the behavioural difference is in **attention routing (QK)**, which showed z up to ±14. But the QK trace used the same badly conditioned (1+γ_in)² weighting, so I need to redo it with the scale-free diagonal-sign statistic. This tells me which heads' \"attend to tokens resembling the query\" structure truly changed, and in which direction, independent of metric. I'll run it on the full RoPE form and on the low-frequency (content) half, with the base for validation.\n\n```python\nL=18;H=4;hd=256;n=640\ndef W(side,l,name): return np.load(f\"/work/np/{side}__model.layers.{l}.{name}.weight.npy\")\nidx_lo=np.r_[64:128,192:256]\ndef stats(M):\n    d=np.diag(M); rn=np.sqrt(np.mean(M**2,axis=1)); cn=np.sqrt(np.mean(M**2,axis=0))\n    nd=d/np.sqrt(rn*cn+1e-30); return np.mean(d>0), np.sum(nd)/np.sqrt(n)\nrec=[]\nfor l in range(L):\n    gi=1+W(\"A\",l,\"input_layernorm\"); sq=1+W(\"A\",l,\"self_attn.q_norm\"); sk=1+W(\"A\",l,\"self_attn.k_norm\")\n    KA,KB=W(\"A\",l,\"self_attn.k_proj\"),W(\"B\",l,\"self_attn.k_proj\"); QA,QB=W(\"A\",l,\"self_attn.q_proj\"),W(\"B\",l,\"self_attn.q_proj\")\n    out=[]\n    for h in range(H):\n        qa,qb=QA[h*hd:(h+1)*hd],QB[h*hd:(h+1)*hd]\n        r=[]\n        for ids in (np.arange(256),idx_lo):\n            BA=(qa[ids].T*(sq*sk)[ids])@KA[ids]; BB=(qb[ids].T*(sq*sk)[ids])@KB[ids]\n            fb,zb=stats((BA+BB)/2); fd,zd=stats(BA-BB); r.append((fb,zb,fd,zd))\n        rec.append((l,h)+r[0]+r[1])\n        out.append(f\"H{h} base {r[0][0]:.2f}/{r[0][1]:+6.1f} D {r[0][2]:.2f}/{r[0][3]:+5.2f} Dlo {r[1][3]:+5.2f}\")\n    print(f\"L{l:2d} \"+\" | \".join(out))\nrec=np.array(rec); np.save(\"/work/qk_signfree.npy\",rec)\nsc=np.load(\"/work/signcopy.npy\")\nprint(\"corr(D_QK z, base OV copy z):\", np.corrcoef(rec[:,5],sc[:,3])[0,1], \" lo:\",np.corrcoef(rec[:,9],sc[:,3])[0,1])\nprint(\"corr(D_QK z, base QK z):\", np.corrcoef(rec[:,5],rec[:,3])[0,1])\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5829,"cache_read_input_tokens":229407,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5829},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8eb70499-b3ff-4738-a5c2-a133122d9167","uuid":"7bfa4ea6-2ee5-491c-80e5-097d3a9eb392","timestamp":"2026-11-07T04:38:53.401Z","request_id":"req_011Cgnx4443sZ3kETU5R73Si"}
